    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Persistence — Create Account: Cloud Account", "technique": "T1136.003", "what_happened": "At 2026-05-13 02:41:08 CEST, an IAM CreateUser call created a new IAM user with the service-masquerading name 'svc-monitoring-20260513_024108' in AWS account 123456789012. The creating principal used stolen admin credentials. No IAM workflow tag was attached (no 'created-by-iga' tag, no ServiceNow/SCM ticket reference, no automation-framework tag) and no permission boundary was set. The service-style username is a Defense Evasion choice — it is designed to blend with legitimate service accounts during identity reviews. Wazuh rule 99310 'AWS: IAM user created without workflow' fired with rule.mitre.id T1136.003.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh rule 99310 fired on a CloudTrail CreateUser event (eventSource iam.amazonaws.com) with no IAM-workflow tag. rule.groups contains aws/amazon/iam. rule.description names the new IAM user svc-monitoring-20260513_024108 and the creating principal.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T02:41:08+0200"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Persistence / Privilege Escalation — admin policy + access key minted", "technique": "T1136.003", "what_happened": "At 02:44:55 CEST (~3m47s after CreateUser), two CloudTrail events fired back-to-back from the same creating principal: AttachUserPolicy attached arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/AdministratorAccess (full AWS admin — a least-privilege violation on a brand-new account), then CreateAccessKey minted access key AKIAIOSFODNN7EXAMPLE with status Active (immediately usable). Wazuh rule 99311 'AWS: AdministratorAccess attached to new user' fired with rule.mitre.id T1136.003. CreateUser + AttachUserPolicy(Admin) + CreateAccessKey all inside ~4 minutes = the 'account factory' pattern the PB054 cloud-account signal describes.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh rule 99311 fired on a CloudTrail AttachUserPolicy event with policyArn ending AdministratorAccess targeting the userName from step 1, immediately followed by a CreateAccessKey event for the same userName. rule.description carries the policy ARN, the new access key ID and the user name. rule.groups contains aws/iam.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T02:44:55+0200"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Discovery / Credential Access — new key used immediately from a different IP", "technique": "T1136.003", "what_happened": "At 02:48:30 CEST (~3m35s after the key was minted), access key AKIAIOSFODNN7EXAMPLE made its first-ever API calls — GetCallerIdentity (sts.amazonaws.com, the standard attacker key-validation step), then ListBuckets (s3.amazonaws.com, returning corp-data / corp-backups / corp-logs / corp-terraform-state) and ListRoles (iam.amazonaws.com, IAM recon for escalation targets). Critically the API calls originated from data.srcip 10.108.57.203 — a DIFFERENT IP than the one that created the user and key. Creator-IP != first-use-IP is the split-operation indicator: stolen creds used from one host, the freshly-minted key exfiltrated and used from the attacker C2. Wazuh rule 99312 'AWS: New IAM key used from different IP' fired with rule.mitre.id T1136.003.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh rule 99312 fired correlating the CreateAccessKey access key ID from step 2 with subsequent CloudTrail API events (GetCallerIdentity, ListBuckets, ListRoles) whose sourceIPAddress (data.srcip 10.108.57.203) differs from the step-1/step-2 creating-principal IP. rule.description names the IAM user, the access key ID and the use-from IP. rule.groups contains aws/iam.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T02:48:30+0200"}
